**Design.** A circular buffer uses a fixed-size array and two indices that wrap around with modulo arithmetic, so enqueue and dequeue are O(1) with no shifting.

Full versus empty is the classic trap: `head == tail` is true for both. Two common fixes: track a `size` counter (simplest), or leave one slot unused.

```java
public class CircularQueue<T> {
    private final Object[] buf;
    private int head = 0;   // index of next element to dequeue
    private int tail = 0;   // index of next free slot
    private int size = 0;

    public CircularQueue(int capacity) {
        if (capacity <= 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException();
        buf = new Object[capacity];
    }
    public boolean enqueue(T x) {
        if (isFull()) return false;
        buf[tail] = x;
        tail = (tail + 1) % buf.length;
        size++;
        return true;
    }
    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public T dequeue() {
        if (isEmpty()) throw new NoSuchElementException();
        T x = (T) buf[head];
        buf[head] = null;          // avoid holding references
        head = (head + 1) % buf.length;
        size--;
        return x;
    }
    public boolean isEmpty() { return size == 0; }
    public boolean isFull()  { return size == buf.length; }
    public int size()        { return size; }
}
```

**Points to say out loud.**
- All operations are O(1) time; space is O(capacity).
- Decide the full policy: reject (return false), throw, block (for producer-consumer), or **overwrite the oldest** (typical for logs and streaming buffers; then advance `head` on overflow).
- Null out dequeued slots so the garbage collector can free objects.
- Capacity could be a power of two so `% n` can become `& (n - 1)`.

**Thread safety.** Make methods `synchronized`, or use `ReentrantLock` with `Condition`s for blocking put/take. For high throughput, a single-producer single-consumer ring buffer can be lock-free with volatile indices.

**Tests:** wraparound after many operations, fill then empty, dequeue on empty, capacity 1.
